<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Workshops</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ateliers nationaux</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Workshops were a public works scheme set up by the Provisional Government of the French Second Republic in February 1848 to give work to the unemployed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Over 100,000 men enrolled, alarming conservatives. Their closure by the elected assembly in June 1848 provoked the June Days uprising, which was crushed by General Cavaignac with thousands killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-workshops.jpg" alt="National Workshops" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ateliers_nationaux_au_Champ-de-Mars_(Bouton).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The workshops answered a crisis of unemployment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Agricultural and commercial distress had prevailed throughout 1847, and the political crisis that ended with the abdication of Louis Philippe added an industrial crisis to it. On 25 February 1848 a group of armed workers interrupted a session of the provisional government to demand the organisation of labour and the right to work.</p><p>Under the influence of one of its members, Louis Blanc, the government passed a decree the same day guaranteeing government-funded work: it undertook &quot;to guarantee the existence of the workmen by work&quot; and &quot;to guarantee work for every citizen&quot;. Blanc wanted a ministry of labour to carry it out, but his colleagues set it aside and instead appointed a labour commission under his presidency, with powers of inquiry and consultation only.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The decree was put into effect by the minister of public works, Marie, and various public works were begun at once. A workman first obtained a certificate from the landlord of his house or lodgings showing his address in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, which the police commissary of the district stamped. He took it to the office of the maire of his ward and received in exchange a note of admission to the national works, giving his name, residence and trade, with which a director of works with vacancies could take him on.</p><p>The system worked while the unemployed numbered fewer than 6,000. Once that number was passed, the men of each arrondissement went from one open work to the next without finding a place and came back to their mayors' offices tired, hungry and discontented.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Workshops” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Workers rose after the government closed the <b>National Workshops</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-23">23 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A provisional government including the poet Alphonse de Lamartine proclaimed the Republic, introduced universal male suffrage, abolished slavery in the colonies under Victor Schoelcher's decree, and created <b>National Workshops</b> for the unemployed.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-second-republic">French Second Republic</a></li></ul></section>
